when germany invaded poland in september  1939 britain and france having pledged  to defend it responded by declaring war  on germany  yet two weeks after german troops  crossed the border from the west  soviet troops invaded from the east and  as many of you will know  there wasn't a response but why why  didn't the allies declare war on the  ussr  so a bit of background throughout the  20s and 30s britain was particularly  concerned about polish  well existence and felt that it  guaranteeing polish independence  would be enough to deter foreign  aggression forever it was not  the british guarantee stated that it  would defend poland against an invasion  by a european power  the treaty did have a secret protocol  though that stated that european power  actually meant germany  and only germany in that in the event of  a soviet invasion britain and poland  would enter into discussions about  mutual assistance  france had also agreed to support poland  and much like britain only agreed to  defend it against german aggression  although france's agreement was much  more informal and was an arrangement  between the french and polish militaries  not their governments and so france  technically had no obligations to poland  whatsoever  a slight snag came when the ussr and  germany signed the molotov ribbon-trop  pact in which they agreed to supply each  other with much-needed materials and  expertise  the pact also secretly divided poland up  between them and also made clear to the  rest of the world that they were going  to economically support each other in  the coming years  so the reason that britain and france  didn't declare war on the ussr were  simple  they didn't have to although there is  some nuance to this  so after britain received word of the  soviet invasion from the east its  reaction was one of caution  the polish ambassador had approached the  british government and was like  maybe help to which britain said no now  this doesn't mean that britain was never  going to help it simply meant that the  uk was weighing its options and  prioritizing victory over germany above  all other things  including poland existing you see the  british government was aware that the  ussr and germany had spent the past half  decade denouncing each other  and now that poland had fallen there was  no buffer state between the two anymore  as such there was a strong belief that  britain and france need only hold out  until the two inevitably turn on each  other the french government held a  similar view but they also believed that  poland as of the soviet invasion no  longer existed  and that france declaring war on stalin  wouldn't do anything to help anyone  and to french strategists this was  actually a good thing since germany now  likely had to keep a large number of  troops on its eastern border meaning  that france would have a much easier job  in defeating germany just like in world  war one  and so when poland was conquered britain  pushed forward with the harshest  measures known  it wrote a strongly worded letter to the  ussr saying that the soviet invasion was  unjustified and frankly  not on in the end both the uk and france  opted to continue relations with the  ussr as if nothing had happened this was  done with the long view in mind  both felt that poland was entirely lost  in that defeating germany was now the  only goal  france stressed that soviet plans would  likely soon clash with german plans and  the two would soon be at each other's  throats for britain the policy was one  of caution and waiting to see what the  soviet  endgame was the soviet union was seen as  a potential future partner whose  industrial might and manpower would  greatly help in defeating germany  and if the cost of that help was east in  poland then france and britain were  happy to accept that  i hope you enjoyed this episode and a  special thanks to my patreon supporters  james bissonette john lucid danny  maloney a man of culture  marvin castell john bisquez mo rob  waterhouse  james castaneda aaron the white jordan  longley gustav swann  marcus azna rasheed ali fielder oink  oink  izzy spinning three plates david  silverman maggie paxkowski  spencer lightfoot winston k wood robert  wetzel anthony beckett lexi schwinn sky  chappelle and kelly moneymaker  also they'd have lost
 

